1) First confirm SHOW track_io_timing is on, otherwise this metric stays at 0 and the rule is meaningless until you enable it;
2) Use pg_stat_statements sorted by shared_blks_read to find the SQL doing the most disk reads, then add indexes or rewrite them;
3) Compare shared_buffers against instance memory — usually about 25% of physical memory;
4) Run iostat -x 1 to confirm whether the disk holding the data directory is saturated.

1) Log in to the host and run systemctl status postgresql or docker ps to confirm the process is alive;
2) If it is alive, connect by hand with psql -h <host> -p <port> -U <user> to tell a hung instance apart from a network or pg_hba authentication problem;
3) If it is not running, read the tail of the log under $PGDATA/log to identify the cause (OOM, full disk, full WAL directory) before starting it;
4) If a streaming replica exists, immediately assess whether to promote it (pg_ctl promote).

1) First confirm SHOW track_io_timing is on, otherwise this metric stays at 0;
2) Check whether buffers_backend in pg_stat_bgwriter is much larger than buffers_checkpoint — if so, shared_buffers is too small or the bgwriter is too conservative;
3) Raise checkpoint_timeout and checkpoint_completion_target to spread the flushing out and avoid IO spikes;
4) Run iostat -x 1 to confirm whether the disk is saturated, and move to faster storage if needed.

1) In postgresql.conf, confirm log_lock_waits=on and a sane deadlock_timeout, then take the full SQL of both sides of the deadlock from the log;
2) Join pg_locks with pg_stat_activity to identify which two tables and which lock modes are waiting on each other;
3) The root cause is usually two transactions updating the same rows in opposite order — enforcing a single locking order in the application removes it;
4) Long transactions amplify deadlocks, so check for transactions that stay open without committing.

1) First rule out a cold cache after a restart (check the instance uptime);
2) Use pg_stat_statements sorted by shared_blks_read to find the SQL doing the most disk reads, and add indexes to eliminate sequential scans first;
3) Compare shared_buffers against instance memory — 25% of physical memory is the usual target;
4) Check whether a full table scan or a batch job is flushing the cache, and move it off peak hours.
